[[eco-overview]]
=== Overview

[abstract]
The Morloc ecosystem has four parts. The *compiler* turns Morloc source into
a build directory. The *runtime library* is linked into every pool and
implements the shared formats. The *nexus* runs a compiled program, from the
command line or as a server. The *environment manager*, `mim`, creates
isolated installations of the other three, solves the foreign packages that
programs need, and serves and freezes the result. This section says what
each part owns, when each runs, where an installation keeps its files, and
how the parts agree on versions.

**Who invokes whom.**

[cols="1,2,2", options="header"]
|===
| Stage | What runs | What it produces
| Provision | `mim` downloads a compiler release and builds an environment; inside it, `morloc init` builds the runtime (<<eco-bootstrap>>) | An installation
| Install | `morloc install` fetches modules (<<eco-modules>>) | Module source in the installation
| Build | `morloc make`, which calls back into `mim` to solve packages (<<eco-solving>>) | A build directory and launchers (<<build-artifacts>>)
| Run | A launcher runs the nexus, which starts the pools (<<build-overview>>) | A result
| Serve | The nexus as a server, started by `mim` (<<eco-serving>>) | MCP tools and HTTP endpoints
| Freeze | `mim` builds an image of the environment (<<eco-freezing>>) | A container image
|===

The compiler and `mim` are coupled only across process boundaries: files
the compiler writes, commands it runs, and environment variables. In every
such contract the compiler (or its release) writes and `mim` reads, so the
burden of compatibility falls on `mim`.

**An installation** is two directory trees. The *home* holds what
`morloc init` builds and is the same for every program; the *state* holds
what accumulates as modules and programs are installed. They are the same
directory unless the state is given its own location.

----
<home>/
|-- bin/           morloc-nexus, and the launcher of each installed program
|-- lib/           the runtime library and per-language libraries
|-- include/       the runtime's C header and per-language headers
|-- opt/           per-language setup state
`-- rust/          the runtime's source, kept for building Rust pools

<state>/
|-- src/morloc/plane/<plane>/<module>/   installed module source
|-- fdb/           one record per installed module, and install locks
|-- modules/       files that modules expose to foreign code
|-- exe/<program>/ installed programs: source copy and build directory
`-- snapshots/     module version pins
----

The home defaults to `~/.local/share/morloc`. Inside an environment, `mim`
gives each environment its own home and state, so nothing is shared between
environments except downloads (<<eco-environments>>).

**Version agreement.** Three rules keep the parts consistent:

 - *Compiler and nexus.* A build's manifest records the compiler's version.
   The nexus runs a manifest only if it was written by the same Morloc
   version (<<build-artifacts>>).
 - *Compiler and runtime.* The runtime is built from the source shipped
   with the compiler's own release, by that compiler, so they agree by
   construction. The runtime library also declares a numeric ABI version,
   which the nexus reports.
 - *Compiler and `mim`.* Each document the compiler hands to `mim` carries
   its own version: the dependency specification an integer, the
   language-support table a `MAJOR.MINOR` schema. A release lists these
   versions in its manifest so that `mim` can refuse, before installing
   anything, a release it cannot read (<<eco-bootstrap>>).

